Practical No. 02: Text Classification using Embedding Layer and LSTM
Objective
Build a binary text classification model using an Embedding layer and LSTM network to classify SMS messages as Ham (normal) or Spam.

Dataset
SMS Spam Collection Dataset

The dataset contains SMS messages labeled as:

Ham – Normal/legitimate message

Spam – Unwanted or fraudulent/promotional message

Technologies Used
Python

Pandas

NumPy

TensorFlow / Keras

Scikit-learn

Matplotlib

Methodology
Load the SMS Spam Collection dataset.

Convert labels into binary values:

Ham = 0

Spam = 1

Clean and preprocess the SMS text.

Split the dataset into training and testing sets.

Tokenize the text using Keras Tokenizer.

Convert text into numerical sequences.

Apply padding to make all sequences the same length.

Build an LSTM model with:

Embedding Layer

LSTM Layer

Dropout Layer

Dense Layer with Sigmoid activation

Train the model for 10 epochs.

Evaluate the model using:

Accuracy

Precision

Recall

F1-Score

Classification Report

Confusion Matrix

Test the model using new SMS messages.

Model Architecture

Input Text
    ↓
Tokenization
    ↓
Padding
    ↓
Embedding Layer
    ↓
LSTM Layer
    ↓
Dropout
    ↓
Dense Layer (Sigmoid)
    ↓
Ham / Spam
Training
The model is trained using:

Optimizer: Adam

Loss Function: Binary Cross-Entropy

Epochs: 10

Batch Size: 32

Validation Split: 20%

Expected Output
For example:


Message: Congratulations! You have won a free prize. Call now!
Predicted Class: Spam
Spam Probability: 0.xxxx
A normal message such as:


Can you send me the notes for today's class?
should be classified as:


Predicted Class: Ham
Evaluation
The performance of the model is evaluated using accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, classification report, and confusion matrix.

The training and validation accuracy/loss graphs are also generated to observe the model's learning performance.

Conclusion
The practical demonstrates how an Embedding layer and LSTM network can be used for binary text classification. The trained model learns patterns in SMS messages and predicts whether a new message belongs to the Ham or Spam class.






